+++ b/doc/source/admin/instance-checkpoints.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,101 @@
+Instance checkpoint model (N1)
+==============================
+
+This private extension provides the journal, ownership and accounting model.
+It does not implement RAM capture or storage snapshots. The production executor
+is unsupported until the separately delivered N2 implementation is installed
+and validated for the intended storage. Fake executors belong to tests only.
+
+Ordinary operation barriers
+---------------------------
+
+The cell Instance row serializes checkpoint admission, ordinary claims and
+identity updates. SQL transactions end before RPC, driver or storage calls.
+Generation 1 has no takeover. Administrative reset, unlock, elapsed time and
+disabling checkpoint creation do not release an owner with an unknown result.
+
+With ``checkpoint.enabled=false`` and no live checkpoint or durable owner, the
+ordinary entrypoint retains its legacy context and does not create a claim.
+The option is not runtime mutable. Activation requires coordinated configuration
+of all writers and a live supported executor; a version number alone is not
+readiness evidence. Previously admitted work remains protected when disabled.
+
+.. list-table:: Entrypoint, first effect and persistent barrier
+   :header-rows: 1
+   :widths: 30 30 40
+
+   * - Entrypoint
+     - First effect
+     - Barrier
+   * - Compute API delete/soft-delete/force-delete/restore; rebuild,
+       resize/confirm/revert, live migration/abort/force-complete,
+       evacuate, shelve/offload/unshelve
+     - Quota, placement, storage/network preparation or task dispatch
+     - Ordinary claim before method body and validation callbacks. Live
+       checkpoints reject topology changes. Existing pending task states remain
+       additional admission barriers.
+   * - Compute API start/stop/reboot, pause/unpause, suspend/resume,
+       image snapshot/backup, crash dump, lock/unlock, network-info injection
+     - State update, Glance image creation or compute RPC
+     - Ordinary claim; these actions can run between checkpoints but cannot
+       overlap an active checkpoint operation. Nested power scopes cannot
+       authorize an attach or another topology operation.
+   * - Volume attach/detach/swap; interface attach/detach; runtime SSH key;
+       allow/deny share; set-admin-password
+     - Attachment creation, reservation, port operation, key CAS or RPC
+     - API claim before external I/O. All device changes remain forbidden while
+       a checkpoint is retained. Key CAS retains its existing optimistic test.
+   * - Server shares controller create/delete
+     - Manila query, ShareMapping write, allow/deny RPC
+     - Guard check before Manila; ShareMapping write joins the Instance lock.
+       A newly created mapping changes the admission fingerprint. The compute
+       API claims before allow/deny effects and hands ownership to compute.
+   * - Cinder volume-snapshot callbacks
+     - Compute snapshot RPC
+     - Claim after BDM/Instance lookup, before dispatch; the worker repeats the
+       check. Partial disk selection is not a checkpoint interface.
+   * - Admin reset-state
+     - InstanceAction and Instance save
+     - Ordinary claim after authorization; active checkpoint/ordinary owners
+       deny the reset. Reset does not clear journal ownership.
+   * - Compute mutation handlers
+     - Driver, attachment, interface, share or migration I/O
+     - Old RPC without a scope acquires before I/O. New asynchronous RPC adopts
+       its queued endpoint/host exactly once and rotates the token. A duplicate
+       delivery cannot adopt a running or completed claim.
+   * - Conductor migrate/rebuild/unshelve and cross-cell confirm/revert
+     - Placement, scheduler, network/storage preparation or compute RPC
+     - Cell targeting precedes ownership. Nested migration tasks cover both
+       execution and rollback. Source and destination cells use separate local
+       scopes; source ownership is never valid in the destination cell.
+   * - Compute live-migration queue
+     - Thread-pool submission, then migration driver
+     - Explicit handoff to ``_do_live_migration`` before submission; the worker
+       adopts before effects. Submission failure retains the queued claim.
+   * - Cell DB Instance/BDM/VIF/ShareMapping/InstanceExtra/info-cache writers
+     - SQL mutation
+     - Same writer transaction locks Instance first. Matching durable scope is
+       required for state changes under ownership; topology cannot change while
+       checkpoints exist. Network status/IP observations and ordinary metadata
+       remain current. Device serial, bus/type/format and related disk properties
+       participate in identity protection.
+
+Only compute and compute-task RPC clients carry the call/cast ownership
+protocol. Synchronous calls borrow the caller's held claim; asynchronous casts
+hand it off. OVO remoting retains its existing conductor protocol and validates
+the internal scope at each DB mutation. Nested OVO arguments use the same scope.
+Scopes are never accepted from HTTP headers or request bodies and are stripped
+from notification contexts. Possession of a scope still requires its current
+cell record, token, action and generation to match. Checkpoint writes additionally
+require a matching claimed phase; only runtime state or reconnection bookkeeping
+is writable, never the protected device identity.
+
+Ordinary failures after acquisition retain the claim for investigation. N1
+provides no automatic unlock or ordinary-worker takeover. Cross-cell workflows
+retain their native pending/resize states and API mapping protocol in addition
+to local claims; a copied Instance row is not a transferable checkpoint owner.
+Runtime proof against real RPC, hypervisor, Neutron and SAN is separate from
+unit SQL/serializer evidence.
+
+The remaining API, recovery and deployment sections are completed with the
+N1 integration and delivery tasks.
